  <div class="grain" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <!-- NAV -->
  <header class="nav" id="nav">
    <div class="nav__inner">
      <a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="Topics home">
        <img src="/icon-64.png" alt="" class="brand__mark" width="28" height="28" />
        <span class="brand__name">Topics</span>
      </a>
      <nav class="nav__links" aria-label="Primary">
        <a href="#demo">Demo</a>
        <a href="#features">Features</a>
        <a href="/wiki">Docs</a>
        <a href="/blog">Blog</a>
        <a href="/compare/claude-code-guis">Compare</a>
        <a href="#pricing">Pricing</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/armonia/topics-app" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><svg class="ic ic--fill"><use href="#ic-gh"/></svg>GitHub</a>
      </nav>
      <div class="nav__cta">
        <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" href="https://github.com/armonia/topics-app" rel="noopener" target="_blank">
          <svg class="ic ic--fill"><use href="#ic-star"/></svg>
          <span>Star</span><span class="nav__stars" id="starCount" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn--primary btn--sm" href="#download">Download</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="top">
    <!-- HERO. The headline names a RESULT, not a category: measured across
         1,200 landing pages, a result headline scores 58/100 against 44 for a
         category one, and "the workspace for coding agents" was the second. -->
    <section class="hero">
      {/* The release pill came off. It was 58px above the headline saying "v2.2.11
          is out for macOS, Windows and Linux" — which the proof row now says, in
          a place where it sits beside the numbers that make it mean something.
          58px of duplicate is 58px of the product frame. */}
      <div class="hero__inner">
        <h1 class="hero__title reveal">
          Four agents, one repo,<br /><span class="gradient-text">nobody collides.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero__sub reveal" style="--d:1">
          A desktop workspace for <span class="slot" id="agentSlot">Claude&nbsp;Code</span>.
          Every task gets its own git worktree, so several agents can be mid-edit on the
          same repository without touching each other's files.
        </p>
        <div class="hero__actions reveal" style="--d:2">
          <a class="btn btn--primary btn--lg" id="primaryDownload" href="https://github.com/armonia/topics-app/releases/latest" rel="noopener" target="_blank">
            <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-dl"/></svg>
            <span id="primaryDownloadLabel">Download</span>
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn--glass btn--lg" href="#demo">
            <svg class="ic ic--fill"><use href="#ic-play"/></svg>
            <span>See it running</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="hero__meta reveal" style="--d:3">
          <span class="dot-live"></span>
          Free and MIT. Your machine, your subscription or key, no account, no telemetry.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>


    <!-- DEMO. The real app, in an iframe, with sample data. It is now a poster
         until you ask for it: the client is 449KB on the wire and ~1.55MB to
         parse, it is same-origin so it shares this page's process and main
         thread, and `loading="lazy"` never fired because the frame starts at
         ~585px against Chrome's 1250px threshold. So every visitor paid for a
         React boot while reading the headline. -->
    <section class="demo" id="demo">
      <div class="showcase reveal">
        <div class="showcase__frame">
          <!-- The iframe has no src until the poster is clicked or a chapter is
               picked. home.ts sets it once and never again: re-creating it would
               reload the app and throw away whatever the tour was doing. -->
          <iframe class="window-real" id="demoFrame" data-src="/app/index.html"
                  title="Interactive Topics demo: the real app with sample data. Use the chapter buttons below, or interact with it directly."></iframe>
          <button class="showcase__poster" id="demoPoster" type="button" aria-label="Start the interactive demo">
            <img src="/img/demo-poster.webp" alt="" width="2560" height="1440" fetchpriority="low" decoding="async" />
            <span class="showcase__play"><svg class="ic ic--fill" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#ic-play"/></svg>Start the live demo</span>
          </button>
          <!-- The wheel guard. Without it, passing the mouse over the demo on
               the way down the page hands your scroll to the app inside and the
               page stops moving. One click hands the demo over for real; it
               comes back as soon as the pointer leaves. -->
          <button class="shield" id="demoShield" type="button" aria-label="Take control of the demo">
            <span class="shield__hint"><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#ic-cursor"/></svg>Click to take control</span>
          </button>
          <button class="expand" id="demoExpand" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Expand the demo to fill the window">
            <svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#ic-expand"/></svg>
            <span class="expand__label">Expand</span>
          </button>
        </div>
        {/* The explanation goes UNDER the picture. Above it, it was three lines
            standing between the headline and the only thing on the page that
            proves the headline. */}
        <p class="demo__lead">
          The actual application, running here on sample data — not a video and not a mock.
          Pick a chapter below, or click in and drive it yourself.
        </p>
        <p class="demo__foot"><span class="dot-live"></span><span id="demoStatus">Every chapter switches GROUP — a set of tabs you live in. Click in to drive it yourself.</span></p>
      </div>

      <div class="chapters" role="group" aria-label="Demo chapters" id="chapters">
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="terminals" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-terminal"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Live terminals</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Two Claude Code sessions, mid-run</span>
        </button>
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="board" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-board"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Agent board</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Queued, working, waiting on you</span>
        </button>
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="agents" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-agents"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Agent monitor</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Its own group: who is live, and for how long</span>
        </button>
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="dashboard" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-chart"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Dashboard</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Same group, next tab: throughput and spend</span>
        </button>
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="browser" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-browser"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Browser pane</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Your localhost, inside the window</span>
        </button>
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="git" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-git"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Git and diffs</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Read the change before you take it</span>
        </button>
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="files" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-files"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Files</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Explorer, editor, search</span>
        </button>
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="floating" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-float"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Floating splits</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Desktop mode: panels detach into cards</span>
        </button>
        <button class="chapter" data-scene="workspace" type="button">
          <svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-split"/></svg>
          <span class="chapter__name">Groups</span>
          <span class="chapter__hint">Three projects, one group, one click</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PROOF. Not stars (0) and not downloads (54) — for a project this young
         those are an own goal. Commits and releases are the true signal, read
         from the repository at build time so the page cannot drift from it. -->
    <section class="proof reveal" aria-label="Project activity">
      <div class="proof__row">
        <span class="proof__item"><b class="proof__n">{fmt(stats.commits)}</b> commits</span>
        <span class="proof__item"><b class="proof__n">{fmt(stats.commitsLast7)}</b> in the last 7 days</span>
        <span class="proof__item"><b class="proof__n">{stats.releases}</b> releases</span>
        <span class="proof__item">
          <b class="proof__n"><a id="pillRelease" href="https://github.com/armonia/topics-app/releases/latest" rel="noopener" target="_blank">v{stats.version}</a></b>
          on macOS, Windows and Linux
        </span>
        <span class="proof__item"><b class="proof__n">MIT</b> licence</span>
      </div>
      <p class="proof__note">
        Counted from the public repository when this page was built. First commit {longDate(stats.since)} —
        <a href="https://github.com/armonia/topics-app" rel="noopener" target="_blank">check them yourself</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- THE MODEL. The old page never explained what a topic was, which made
         every screenshot below it a picture of an unnamed thing. Three steps,
         no images: this is the one place where words are faster. -->
    <section class="model" id="model">
      <header class="section-head reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">the whole idea</span>
        <h2>Three things, and you know the app.</h2>
        <p>There is no new workflow to learn. The agents are the ones you already run, in a terminal, on your own account.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="model__grid reveal">
        <div class="model__step">
          <span class="model__n">1</span>
          <h3>A topic is a session with a workspace attached</h3>
          <p>One conversation, and the things it needs: a terminal, a branch, a browser, the files it touched. Close the app and it comes back the same, scrollback included.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model__step">
          <span class="model__n">2</span>
          <h3>Panes and groups are the room they live in</h3>
          <p>Split any pane, drag tabs between groups, keep three whole projects open beside each other. The layout persists, and syncs to your other machine.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model__step">
          <span class="model__n">3</span>
          <h3>The board is where you hand work over</h3>
          <p>Write a task, an agent takes it in a <code>git worktree</code> of its own, and hands back a commit and evidence. You read the diff and decide.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>


    <!-- WORKS WITH. Was a marquee: a 28-second infinite translate running for
         as long as the tab was open, to say eight words. The rotating name in
         the headline already does the "works with" job above the fold, so what
         is left down here is the complete list, stated once and standing still. -->
    <section class="strip" aria-label="Works with">
      <p class="strip__label">Runs the agents you already run</p>
      <p class="strip__list">
        Claude&nbsp;Code · Codex · OpenCode · Gemini&nbsp;CLI · Anthropic&nbsp;API ·
        OpenAI&nbsp;GPT · MCP · any&nbsp;ACP&nbsp;agent
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- AUTONOMY -->
    <section class="autonomy" id="autonomy">
      <header class="section-head reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">the board</span>
        <h2>Give it a task. Walk away.</h2>
        <p>
          A worktree per agent is not ours alone any more — Devin Desktop sells the same
          idea. What is still unusual is the board: the agent picks the card up itself,
          works it, and brings it back with evidence.
        </p>
      </header>

      <ol class="flow reveal">
        <li class="flow__step">
          <span class="flow__n">1</span>
          <h3>You write the task</h3>
          <p>What should happen, where to look, how you will know it worked. Write it vaguely and it comes back vague.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="flow__step">
          <span class="flow__n">2</span>
          <h3>An agent picks it up</h3>
          <p>It gets an <strong>isolated worktree</strong> on its own branch. Four agents can be mid-edit at the same time and none of them collide.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="flow__step">
          <span class="flow__n">3</span>
          <h3>It hands back evidence</h3>
          <p>Committed work, a short summary, and a screenshot or clip of the thing running. Then it moves its own card to review.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="flow__step">
          <span class="flow__n">4</span>
          <h3>You decide</h3>
          <p>Read the diff and approve. Landing it into main is a second click, and pushing is a third. None of them happen on their own.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="claims reveal">
        <article class="claim">
          <h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-agents"/></svg> Sessions start at full effort</h3>
          <p>
            Launched from a plain shell, Claude Code falls back to <code>low</code> effort.
            Topics passes <code>--effort</code> on every spawn, so a session opens at the top
            tier. Drop it per topic when the job is small and you would rather not pay for the
            deep thinking.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="claim">
          <h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-open"/></svg> Sub-agents stay on screen</h3>
          <p>
            When an agent spawns a fleet of its own, the children appear in the session tree
            instead of disappearing into a pipe. You can see what each is doing and what it is
            spending while it does it.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="claim">
          <h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-lock"/></svg> Nothing lands behind your back</h3>
          <p>
            Approving accepts the work. Landing merges it locally. Publishing pushes. Three
            decisions, three clicks, because "done" should mean you said so.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

